{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<p class="crumb"><a href="{{backUrl}}">&larr; Mail</a></p>
<h1>{{heading}}</h1>

<form class="editor" method="post" action="{{backUrl}}">
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{csrf}}">
  <input type="hidden" name="action" value="save">
  {{#editing}}<input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{form_id}}">{{/editing}}

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-name">what this rule is for</label>
    <input id="r-name" type="text" name="name" value="{{form_name}}" placeholder="my wife&rsquo;s mail">
    <p class="hint">A note to yourself. It is what the mail log shows beside every message this
      rule decided.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-to">address it matches</label>
    <input id="r-to" type="text" name="to" value="{{form_to}}" placeholder="{{everyone}}" required>
    <p class="hint">The name before the <code>@</code>, or <code>{{everyone}}</code> for every
      address at {{domain}}. A catch-all matches everything below it too, so give it a high
      position number.</p>
    {{#anyAddresses}}<p class="hint">Named here:
      {{#addresses}}<code>{{localPart}}</code> {{/addresses}}</p>{{/anyAddresses}}
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-from">only when the sender contains</label>
    <input id="r-from" type="text" name="from" value="{{form_from}}" placeholder="(anything)">
    <p class="hint">Optional. Matched against the envelope sender, which is what the connection
      said rather than what the message claims about itself.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-subject">only when the subject contains</label>
    <input id="r-subject" type="text" name="subject" value="{{form_subject}}" placeholder="(anything)">
    <p class="hint">Optional. A subject is chosen by whoever sent the message, so this is a
      convenience and never a control.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-what">then</label>
    <select id="r-what" name="what">
      <option value="forward"{{#isForward}} selected{{/isForward}}>send it on</option>
      <option value="deliver"{{#isDeliver}} selected{{/isDeliver}}>keep it here, in a mailbox</option>
      <option value="drop"{{#isDrop}} selected{{/isDrop}}>accept it and let it go</option>
    </select>
    <p class="hint"><strong>Keeping it</strong> puts the message in the mailbox of whoever owns
      that address, where they read it at <code>/self/mail</code> and get a notification. It needs
      a named address with an owner, so it cannot be a catch-all.</p>
    <p class="hint"><strong>Dropping</strong> accepts the message and tells the sender it arrived,
      which is the honest answer for mail somebody simply does not want &mdash; refusing would tell
      them their address is wrong when it is right.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-forward">send it to</label>
    <input id="r-forward" type="email" name="forwardTo" value="{{form_forwardTo}}" placeholder="her@gmail.com">
    <p class="hint">Only for &ldquo;send it on&rdquo;. Delivered to that domain&rsquo;s own mail
      exchangers, over TLS, with the message untouched below the headers this server adds. It
      cannot be an address at {{domain}}, which would send the message back through this same
      rule.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="r-position">position</label>
    <input id="r-position" type="number" name="position" value="{{form_position}}" min="1" max="9999">
    <p class="hint">Lower is consulted first. Leave the specific rules low and the catch-all
      high.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="enabled"{{#form_enabled}} checked{{/form_enabled}}> this rule is on</label>
  </div>

  <div class="doing"><button type="submit">Save</button></div>
</form>

{{#editing}}
<form method="post" action="{{backUrl}}" class="danger">
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{csrf}}">
  <input type="hidden" name="action" value="delete">
  <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{form_id}}">
  <button type="submit">Delete this rule</button>
</form>
{{/editing}}

<div class="card">
  <p class="meta"><span class="dim">what happens to a message this rule forwards</span></p>
  <p class="hint">Nothing below the headers is changed &mdash; no footer, no subject tag, no
    re-encoding &mdash; because the sender&rsquo;s own DKIM signature covers the body and breaks if
    one byte moves.</p>
  <p class="hint">A <code>Received</code> line, an ARC seal and a signature of this domain&rsquo;s
    are added on the front. The return path is rewritten to an address here so it passes SPF at the
    far end; bounces come back to it and are unwrapped and sent on to whoever wrote the message.</p>
  <p class="hint">Whatever the destination says is passed straight back to the sending server. A
    &ldquo;try later&rdquo; becomes a try later, a refusal becomes a refusal &mdash; so there is no
    queue here and nothing to lose in a restart.</p>
</div>
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
